  "iterations": 3,
  "final_answer": "Here's the **on-time ship rate** by region for 2026 year-to-date (Jan\u2013Aug 2026, the latest data available), per the metric definition: *\"The share of orders shipped on or before their committed ship date (order_date plus standard lead time), out of all orders with a non-cancelled status.\"*\n\n| Region | 2026 YTD avg on-time rate |\n|---|---|\n| Midwest | ~97.4% |\n| Northeast | ~97.1% |\n| West | ~96.8% |\n| Pacific NW | ~96.7% |\n| Southwest | ~96.7% |\n| Southeast | ~96.4% |\n\nAll six regions ran at essentially **100% on-time** every month from March 2025 through July 2026 \u2014 the regional averages above are pulled down almost entirely by a single month.\n\n**Notable pattern:** In **August 2026**, every region's rate dropped sharply and simultaneously:\n- Southeast: 71.3%\n- Southwest: 73.7%\n- Pacific NW: 73.9%\n- West: 74.2%\n- Northeast: 76.6%\n- Midwest: 79.0%\n\nBecause the dip hits all regions in the same month by a similar magnitude (rather than one region trending down over time), this looks like a fleet-wide operational disruption in August 2026 (e.g., a shared logistics/capacity constraint) rather than a region-specific fulfillment problem. If you'd like, I can dig into whether specific accounts, tiers, or press-line/production delays concentrated in that month are driving it \u2014 the region grain alone doesn't point to a single root cause the way a shared batch/lot/supplier would for a quality metric.",
  "model": "claude-sonnet-5",
